  "answer": "I'll look through the Shopizer storefront cart API and how store merchant settings are read so the per-line quantity cap can sit in the right place.The storefront cart writes quantity in three places. I\u2019ll cap each of those against the store\u2019s `CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY`, read on every request, and reject the change before the cart is saved.The storefront cart now rejects any create, add, or quantity change that would put more than N units on a line. N comes from the store merchant setting `CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY` and defaults to 99 when that setting is missing. The value is read from the database on each request, so a change applies without a restart. A quantity of 0 still removes the line. Requests over the limit return HTTP 400 and leave the cart unchanged. This covers `POST /api/v1/cart`, `PUT /api/v1/cart/{code}`, and `POST /api/v1/cart/{code}/multi`.\n\nVERIFICATION: {\"ran\": [\"mvn -pl sm-shop -am compile -DskipTests -q\"], \"result\": \"not-run\", \"could_not_verify\": [\"HTTP 400 responses and that the cart is unchanged when a line would exceed CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY, including the default of 99 and a live config change\"], \"missing\": [\"a cart API test that sets merchant configuration CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY and calls the storefront cart endpoints\"]}",
